            <ArticleTitle>Effects of a Yoga Program on Menstrual Cramps and Menstrual Distress in Undergraduate Students with Primary Dysmenorrhea: A Single-Blind, Randomized Controlled Trial.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>732-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1089/acm.2016.0058</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate the effects of a yoga program on menstrual cramps and menstrual distress in undergraduate students with primary dysmenorrhea.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Single-blind, randomized controlled trial.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">40 randomly selected undergraduate nursing students, with 20 each assigned to an exercise or a control group.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">The participants engaged in a yoga program for 60 minutes once a week for 12 weeks. The program consisted of physical exercise combined with relaxation and meditation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Menstrual cramps and menstrual distress levels were measured by using the Visual Analogue Scale for Pain and the Menstrual Distress Questionnaire, respectively. Data were analyzed by using the Kolmogorov-Smirnov and Shapiro-Wilk normality tests, t-test, chi-square test, logistic regression analysis, and multivariate analysis of variance (SPSS program).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Menstrual pain intensity (group difference, -0.94; 95% confidence interval [CI], -1.47 to -0.42; p = 0.001) and menstrual distress (group difference, -1.13; 95% CI, -1.43 to -0.82; p &lt; 0.0001) scores decreased significantly in the experimental group compared with the control group.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">These findings indicate that yoga interventions may reduce menstrual cramps and menstrual distress in female undergraduate students with primary dysmenorrhea.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
